WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.960
Help all kids learn and grow with PBS Kids.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.220
Thank you for supporting your PBS station.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.320
On the South American continent,

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.740
in the largest rainforest in the world, the Amazon.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.660
We're here on the rainforest floor.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.280
It's us, the Krapp brothers.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:26.160
I'm Martin.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.190
I'm Chris.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.970
A wet, humid rainforest is a great-

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.940
It's a great place to go looking for frogs.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.040
So let's split up, spread out, and see what we can find.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.720
Look, meet the giant, broad-headed tree frog.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:47.090
This is a big tree frog.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:48.990
And look at that wide head.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.450
This frog has amazing tree climbing powers.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:53.490
Look at those feet.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.780
See the suction pad tips?

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.640
Those are great for gripping while climbing.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:01.690
Show us the jump climb.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:04.680
Amazing!

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.120
There's one.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.170
Whoa, look at this awesome frog.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:15.780
It's so colorful.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.250
I love the bright green stripes.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:19.590
Martin, check out this one.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.360
Dendrobates family.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.600
This family is full of some of the most colorful frogs

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:27.300
in the world.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.260
Oh yeah, all different colors.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:29.880
Blue.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.540
reds, oranges, yellows, and greens.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.000
Every color under the rainbow.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.290
Those lines really stand out.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:41.030
And check it out, it's got a little blue on the legs underneath.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.310
I like it. I like this frog.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.380
What an awesome Dendrobates frog.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.560
What are those colors for?

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.440
Imagine if we could do what these frogs can do.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:56.520
Imagine if we had the creature powers

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.580
of the Dendrobates frogs.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:59.920
We have all the colors of that beautiful Amazon rain.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:07.820
Remember, all these transmitter chips need to be a different color.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.850
So they can't all be blue, Martin.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.820
Aw, well, this one will be.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.460
Why do you guys like blue and green so much anyway?

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.650
Green reminds me of nature.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.320
All the forests, jungles and grasslands,

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:23.580
where I love to be.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:28.890
Blue is like the water and the sky and my eyes.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:34.620
And besides, blue is a primary color for paints.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.180
Green is just a secondary color.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:39.260
Yeah.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:43.360
Well, green is better because it's a mix of two paint colors.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.320
Yellow and blue.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.800
That's way more interesting.

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:52.350
It's true.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:54.110
But you did use blue.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:55.170
Right.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.130
But color is pretty complex.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.910
When you're talking about light as opposed to pink,

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.800
Then green becomes a primary color.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.320
Green and red light makes yellow.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:08.170
Well, so I guess it comes down to this.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:09.900
All colors are cool.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:11.000
Oh, yeah.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.630
Oh, paint on this frog.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:16.590
I didn't do that.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:18.900
A poison frog.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:20.250
He's fine.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:21.650
That's just how they look.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.080
They are so colorful.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.010
Wow, he's beautiful.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.970
Yeah, this one's called the cobalt po-

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.200
scientific name, Dendrobates tinctureus.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:37.380
There are so many different kinds of poison frogs,

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.240
all with their own brilliant colors.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:40.920
Wow. Ooh.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.960
Yep, all the colors of the rainbow.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.480
Red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.680
Hey, you did that without looking.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:51.680
Yep, Roy-J-Biv.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:52.960
Say what?

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.200
Roy-J-Biv. That's how I remember it.

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:59.970
Red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:01.360
Roy G. Biv.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:02.620
Cool.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:04.320
That's what I'll name you.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.180
Roy G. Biv.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.940
First name Roy, middle initial G, last name Biv.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.320
Roy G. Biv.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:14.810
Think he likes it.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:17.900
Okay, shaft ready.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:21.830
Just load the transmitter in here and we're ready to do some science.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:23.490
Okay, great.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:29.360
In order to study the home range of squirrel monkeys, we need to get those transmitters onto...

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:36.440
those monkeys. Squirrel monkeys live in big groups that sometimes break off into smaller groups.

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:41.120
Be kind of confusing, but if we track them, we can try to understand them better.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.080
But how do we get the transmitters onto the monkeys?

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.670
The research blow dart is the perfect method.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:57.260
Special dart sticks harmlessly to the monkey's fur.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:05:59.970
Now that little transmitter sends...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.420
signal to our tablet and we can see his movements on the map even if we can't see

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:10.350
him awesome let me get the next one with my yellow transmitter

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:41.290
Hey, little monkey.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:42.220
Oh, no.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:42.720
No, no, no.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:43.200
Little monkey.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:44.240
Don't press that.

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:51.230
She miniaturized us.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:06:59.850
Hey, go.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.280
That's not a toy.

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:01.870
Yeah.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.490
We really kind of need that.

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:07.960
So don't even think about running off with it.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:14.560
No, that monkey made off with our miniaturizer.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:16.760
If we don't get that miniaturizer back,

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:18.870
we'll be mini-sized forever.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:21.150
We gotta catch up to that monkey.

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:23.610
Is the transmitter working, Cookie?

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:24.770
Yes.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:26.540
At least we can track our movements.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:27.480
Okay.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:29.420
We'll go after the miniaturizer.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:33.380
And Jimmy and I will hug that idea.

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.110
This is so big size.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:42.740
We're going to get around in here.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.900
I don't know, but we've got to try.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:53.430
Oh, hey, Roy.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:54.390
Gee.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:55.340
Biff.

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:59.970
This is such a giant forest when you're such small.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:01.360
Isn't it, Roy?

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:03.290
Yeah.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.440
Roy definitely has small creature experience.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:08.320
Maybe we should stick with him?

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.560
Well, he is moving in the direction of the miniaturizer.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:14.120
Oh, I wish we had a creature power suit.

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:15.950
Maybe Aviva can make us one.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:17.270
Let's call her.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.400
my old popsicle stick here.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:33.390
Okay, get on.

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:36.480
Ready?

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:42.220
Ready, Tortuga here.

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:47.170
Aviva, we could really use a poison frog power suit.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:48.330
Can you make one?

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:54.460
We need a creature power boost.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:56.710
We can't cover any ground here.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:58.120
It's our only chance.

00:08:58.830 --> 00:08:59.710
Okay, I'll try.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:02.460
Just don't let Roy out of your sight, then.

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.180
We won't. Follow that rainbow!

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:10.080
Okay, Jimmy. Let's get to work.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:13.100
Righty-o. But I'm gonna need a snack.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:18.870
Why are we stopping?

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.380
Whoa! It's an ant crossing.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:26.350
Amazing! A snack for a poison frog.

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:29.070
How's that for a sticky-tongued wide-mouth attack?

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:33.590
Thanks, Roy. I did not want to get run over by an ant.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:37.010
No! Stop! Don't touch him!

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:38.720
He has poison skin.

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:40.110
Poison skin?

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:42.830
Yeah, that's why they're called poison frogs.

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:48.510
Some types of poison frogs have enough poison in their skin to kill 20,000 mice.

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:51.810
And much smaller than mouse size now.

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:54.670
Yeah, it's a great defense against predators.

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:58.100
I'll say. But where does the poison come from?

00:09:58.560 --> 00:09:59.700
From the food they eat.

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:04.050
Ants, termites, and millipedes have certain chemicals

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:07.830
that the poison frogs collect in glands inside their bodies.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:12.200
These glands concentrate the chemicals and produce the poison.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.880
That means you better eat up, Roy.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.560
We don't want any predators to get you.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:20.840
Okay, Jimmy.

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:28.040
A little more.

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:29.560
We have a lot of stretchy...

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:43.000
A lot harder making a suit in Minnie.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.760
I'm going to ask them to get me a DNA sample.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:48.960
Some skin or slime, that would make it a lot easier.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:55.290
Where are you going, Roy?

00:10:56.650 --> 00:10:58.470
Whoa, you gotta see this!

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.170
What? It's a nest of unhatched tadpoles!

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:05.550
They must belong to Roy and his mate.

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:06.410
Cool!

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:09.980
And Roy has been guarding them ever since the eggs were laid.

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:11.510
That's one of his jobs.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:16.360
And making sure they stay nice and moist.

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:22.120
Hey! Maybe we can take just a little dollop of that slime for a Viva sample.

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:23.550
Yes, of course!

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:29.980
Sending a Viva scan...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.460
She's got it!

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.760
Hey, the tadpoles, they're moving!

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:37.800
They're beginning to hatch!

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:38.840
One's out!

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:41.660
Welcome to the world, little tadpole.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:43.100
Happy birthday!

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:45.500
Oh, wow, these tadpoles need names.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:48.040
I'll name them after the colors of the rainbow.

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:56.040
You'll be red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, and violet.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:02.460
A froggy back ride!

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:04.330
A froggy back ride?

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:06.680
Kind of like a piggy back ride.

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:09.580
But pigs don't carry their babies on their backs.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:11.180
These frogs do!

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:13.630
We gotta see where he's taking them.

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:16.670
The rest of you Tad Tolls, wait here, okay?

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:18.530
Hurry, he's climbing!

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:20.510
Climb, too?

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:22.550
I love these frogs!

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:25.210
I hope Aviva hurries with that suit!

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.220
Climbing mini-sized isn't gonna be easy!

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:29.980
This slime...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:30.920
My program sample analyzed,

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:36.160
my programming is almost complete!

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:53.040
Definitely! He's fast!

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:54.330
For sure!

00:12:58.520 --> 00:12:59.500
Portation ready!

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:25.940
Did it!

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:28.680
Good thing I'm the climbing brother.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:29.300
Yeah!

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.480
And since you got it first, activate, bro.

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:35.110
Insert Poison Frog Power Disc.

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:39.070
Touch frog with glove.

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:42.420
And activate Poison Frog Power.

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:07.630
It's what happens when I'm rushed. I gotta work on that. The poison isn't active either. Remember, you'd have to eat ants for that.

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:18.460
I'll pass. But let's see what these frog legs can do. Whoa, wow, these legs can really launch you far.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:22.870
Oh, I love leaping with frog legs. Cool leaps.

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:25.520
Yeah. Hey, look, Roy's taking off.

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:29.940
Can you give us a lift? All aboard for a froggy back ride.

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:32.300
Wait for us, Roy!

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:40.760
Are high enjoying the ride?

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:45.160
Well, froggynack rides are pretty bouncy.

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:46.680
Whoa!

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:49.600
Flippery!

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:54.050
It's amazing that those tadpoles stick on so well.

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:57.790
Hey, looks like that monkey troop is close by.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:14:59.760
There's one!

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:02.670
Those monkeys eat small insects, lizards, and amphibians.

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:04.060
Roy, look out!

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:05.950
She sees him!

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:09.650
Oh, why is Roy so bright and easy to see?

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:11.520
Uh-oh.

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:18.000
Good one, Mom.

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:21.210
She's teaching him an important lesson of the forest.

00:15:21.470 --> 00:15:25.490
Oh, yeah, she's saying don't touch those bright, colorful frogs.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:26.500
They're poisonous.

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:27.990
Amazing!

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:29.970
So that's why they're co-

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:32.580
Yup, a warning sign.

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:35.840
One that's bright, obvious, and easy to remember.

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:38.790
It's an awesome defense called warning coloration.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:41.920
So if you're a poisonous frog,

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:45.520
you want bright colors that'll stand out as a warning to predators.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:46.480
Exactly.

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:50.700
But if you don't have poison, you want to blend in and hide from predators.

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:51.880
Like my suit.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:54.680
It doesn't have poison or colors, so...

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.340
Uh-oh! So we're in trouble!

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:06.120
Hop, Chris! Hop!

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:07.300
I'm hopping!

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:14.780
Come in! Mayday, mayday!

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:16.200
We need color! Quick!

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:27.010
Poison, just the color!

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:29.390
Yeah, just the color! The color!

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:32.650
If you can get the color working, we can fool the monkey.

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:37.270
They know that Brightfrogs are dangerous and they think they're poisonous, even if we're not.

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:39.260
It's called mimicry.

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:40.760
Yeah, mimicry!

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:48.840
Butterflies are poisonous, but Viceroy butterflies aren't.

00:16:49.310 --> 00:16:52.690
Viceroy's just look like monarchs so that predators think they're poisonous.

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:57.630
Same with the non-venomous milk snake, who looks like the venomous coral snake.

00:16:58.500 --> 00:16:59.000
Mimics!

00:16:59.640 --> 00:16:59.880
Yeah!

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:02.700
So just get us that color! We need color!

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:04.410
Okay, I'm hurry.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:14.850
Oh, we're cornered. This is not gonna end well.

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:20.830
Update suit. Please work.

00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:29.980
I have a yellow and...

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:34.360
Oh, you look cool, bro.

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:35.900
And just in time.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:37.380
Way to go!

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:39.660
Now they'll leave Chris alone.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:42.630
There's the monkey with the miniaturizer.

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:44.400
Oh, hey, wait, wait, wait.

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:46.180
Can we have our miniaturizer back?

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:46.970
Please!

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:48.460
Uh-oh.

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:49.900
Uh-oh.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:54.120
Listen, there's something in there.

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:57.020
Geez!

00:17:57.480 --> 00:17:57.640
Boo!

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:02.570
What are you doing in there?

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:06.400
Wow!

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:13.720
Cool plants called bromeliads gather rainwater.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:16.100
It's like a hidden mini swimming pool

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:17.970
in the middle of the forest.

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:20.480
Look, Little Red is going in.

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:23.580
Oh, this is awesome.

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:25.700
